DOCUMENT();

############################################################
# Load Macros
############################################################
loadMacros(
  "PGstandard.pl",
  "PGML.pl",
  "AnswerFormatHelp.pl",
  "PCCmacros.pl",
  "parserImplicitEquation.pl",
  "contextCurrency.pl",
  "PGcourse.pl",
);
COMMENT('Authored in PreTeXt');

############################################################
# Header
############################################################
TEXT(beginproblem());


############################################################
# PG Setup Code
############################################################
$m = random(10, 90, 5);
$mdecimal = $m/100;
$wdecimal = random(5, 20, 0.05);
Context("ImplicitEquation");
Context()->variables->are(s => 'Real');
$r = 1 - $mdecimal;
$answerdecimal = Real($wdecimal/$r);
Context()->variables->set(s => {limits=>[$answerdecimal - 100, $answerdecimal + 100]});
$equation = ImplicitEquation("s(1 - $mdecimal) = $wdecimal");
Context("Currency")->flags->set(trimTrailingZeros => 1);
$w = Currency($wdecimal);
$answer = Currency($answerdecimal);

############################################################
# Body
############################################################

BEGIN_PGML
In retail, an item has a wholesale price [`w`] that the store pays to obtain the item. The shelf price [`s`] is what a customer pays to buy the item. The "markup factor" [`m`] is a number that explains what proportion of the shelf price is profit. For example if the markup factor is [`0.15\text{,}`] it means that [`15\%`] of the shelf price is profit for the store. These numbers are related by the formula [`s(1-m)=w\text{.}`]

Suppose the markup is [`[$m]\%`] and the wholesale price is [`[$w]\text{.}`] Write an equation that could be used to find the shelf price. Then find the shelf price.

[@KeyboardInstructions('Write the equation here.')@]**

[_]{$equation}{15}

[@KeyboardInstructions('Write the shelf price here.')@]**

[_]{$answer}{10}


END_PGML

############################################################
# Solution
############################################################

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
We start with the equation [`s(1-m)=w`] and substitute in what we know. The value for [`m`] should be [`[$mdecimal]`] and the value for [`w`] should be [`[$wdecimal]\text{.}`] So we have the equation

[```\newcommand{\amp}{&}
\begin{aligned}
s(1-[$mdecimal]) \amp= [$wdecimal]\\
s([$r]) \amp= [$wdecimal]
\end{aligned}
```]

Now to solve for [`s\text{,}`] divide on each side by [`[$r]\text{:}`]

[```\newcommand{\divideunder}[2]{\frac{#1}{{\highlight{{#2}}}}}\newcommand{\amp}{&}
\begin{aligned}
\divideunder{s([$r])}{[$r]} \amp= \divideunder{[$wdecimal]}{[$r]}\\
s \amp= [$answerdecimal]
\end{aligned}
```]

So the shelf price is [$answer].


END_PGML_SOLUTION

############################################################
# End Problem
